      "2001.159.003\nCLIPPER SHIP \"FLYING CLOUD\"\nThe front cover of this menu is a reproduction of an authentic Currier \u0026 Ives clipper ship print\nCurrier \u0026 Ives, known as the \"Printmakers of America\", graphically preserved the glorious clipper\nship era in a magnificent series of clipper ship prints. This print, one of a series, is being reproduced\nthrough the courtesy of The Museum of the City of New York\nLaunched in 1851, the \"Flying Cloud' was the largest merchant ship afloat. In her brief career\nthe Flying Cloud\" was perhaps the greatest and the tallest of the China clippers. She set a half\ndozen of the world's permanent sailing records, and reaped huge profits, once making nearly $200,000\nten me ran Car t Flan Cd sped to the Golden Gate\nin 89 days. Thereafter eighty-nine was the magic number for every other clipper captain.\nLike Pan Am's modern Clippers of the air, the early chpper ships traveled the lanes of com\nmerce to all the tar places of the world... to Japan.\n•. to Australia... and, in the days of the great\ngold rush, from America's east coast, around Cape Horn, South America, to California. Our current\nequipment.\nthe Clipper fleet of the wentieth Century\n••. consists Ol the hnest commercial\naircraft ever developed\n-aircraft that have helped us establish an unparalleled record for safety.\nspeed and service.\n*CLIPPER TRADE MARK-REG US PAT OFFICE - COPYRIGHT 1966 PAN AMERICAN WORLD AIRWAYS ING\nLITHO IN USA -8739-0393"
